              <td height="480"> <font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><font face="verdana"> 
                </font> 
                </font><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> </font><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 
                  <br>
                  PMWATCH -- February 6, 2002</font> 
                </font>
                </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">NPR's Ombudsman, 
                  Jeffrey Dvorkin, is once again, it seems, giving his listeners 
                  the run around and acting NOT as a watchdog for NPR's listeners 
                  -- as his job requires him to do -- but as a buffer, a protective 
                  belt, a protector of NPR from its troublesome and noisy listenership.</font></p>
                <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Below is 
                  an Activism Update from FAIR pointing out that NPR, despire 
                  complaints that go back at least to early January (see <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/cast/racistcalm.asp"><font size="2">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/cast/racistcalm.asp 
                  </font></a>), continues to talk about "a period of relative 
                  calm", even though Palestinians continue to be killed, 
                  day in and day out (as 5 Palestinians were assassinated today). 
                  Instead of taking seriously the constant stream of complaints 
                  (PMWatchers have been sending in complaints on this issue on 
                  a regular basis since Jaunary 3, 2002), and acting on them, 
                  Mr. Dvorkin seems to be happy telling us that, yes, we are right, 
                  that yes, we have a point, and that please, make sure we write 
                  him again and tell him how we feel about their coverage.</font></p>
                <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">What good 
                  are Mr. Dvorkin's promises if he is not going to take them seriously, 
                  and how genuine is his statement that he agrees that Palestinian 
                  deaths are as tragic as Israeli deaths?</font></p>
                <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Please remind 
                  Jeffrey Dvorkin, once again, and again, that NPR cannot go around 
                  equating "peace" with peace for Israelis only.</font></p>
                <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">You can 
                  contact Jeffrey Dvorkin, NPR's Ombudsman, at ombudsman@npr.org 
                  and/pr: 202.513.2000 -- you can also reach him by filling the 
                  form below and pressing "send".</font> </p>
